UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 13, Lesson 2: Making sounds with instruments', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Musical instruments make sound when a part vibrates: strings, skins, air columns or solid bars.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMatch instrument→vibrating part: violin; bongo; xylophone bar.\n\nFEEDBACK\nString; skin; solid bar (accept air for wind if named wind instrument instead).\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nClassify eight instruments by what vibrates. Build one simple instrument from scrap and label the vibrating part.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Musical instruments make sound when a part vibrates: strings, skins, air columns or solid bars.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and make","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Classify eight instruments by what vibrates. Build one simple instrument from scrap and label the vibrating part. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about instrument linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string.. Correction: String; skin; solid bar (accept air for wind if named wind instrument instead). with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses instrument. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about instrument, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about instrument. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for instrument. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for instrument. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","String; skin; solid bar (accept air for wind if named wind instrument instead)","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string"],"question":"Match instrument→vibrating part: violin; bongo; xylophone bar. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for instrument; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string","Musical instruments make sound when a part vibrates: strings, skins, air columns or solid bars","String; skin; solid bar (accept air for wind if named wind instrument instead)","Careful use of vibrate and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with instrument?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named vibrate and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string","Name vibrate and explain why it matters only, without linking it to instrument","Apply instrument to new material and name the vibrate and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies instrument with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string"],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which claim about instrument is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string","A named observation plus how it supports the claim"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about instrument?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about instrument with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","String; skin; solid bar (accept air for wind if named wind instrument instead)","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Using this science idea — Musical instruments make sound when a part vibrates: strings, skins, air columns or solid bars. — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about instrument?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any exciting story about the object must be true","Saying “the music comes from electricity” for an acoustic guitar ignores the vibrating string","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about instrument should change with it"],"question":"Which prediction about instrument can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_ch